WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:13.290
Located between the Pacific Ocean and the
Caribbean Sea, we could say Colombia is something

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:16.610
like the entry and exit door of South America.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:17.610
And...

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.230
You know what?

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:20.230
This is Colombia…

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:25.490
The long and cheerful beaches of Santa Marta,
La Guajira Desert [Pronounced: la wha-hee-ra];

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:29.810
the inaccessible Leticia, capital of the leafy
Colombian Amazon rainforest…

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:34.370
Them there are the islands of San Andres and
Providencia, the coffee belt of Quindío,

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:40.530
Caldas and Risaralda, and, of course, the
vibrant cities of Medellin, Cali, or Barranquilla.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:41.530
And…

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:49.060
We can’t forget about the historical and
proud Cartagena or the frenetic Bogota - the

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:52.420
great capital city of the country that, dear
viewers, with its Candelaria, its Cerro de

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:56.059
Monserrate, or it’s incredible museums of
gold and art, will leave you speechless.

00:00:56.059 --> 00:00:57.059
Yes...

00:00:57.059 --> 00:01:02.690
We can’t really be surprised that more and
more travel guides are making Colombia a 100%

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:03.690
must-visit destination.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:09.320
But as you may have guessed, this video is
not going to be about tourism, not at all…

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.450
It’s politics that we do here, after all.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:22.130
On May 2018 the next presidential election
in Colombia will be held, and by August 7th

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:28.810
this country will have a new President because
the current one, Juan Manuel Santos, has already

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:31.340
reached the limit of two terms.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:35.399
So, as Santos’ time is coming to an end,
with this video we want to take a look ahead

00:01:35.399 --> 00:01:39.369
and find out what kind of Colombia he is going
to leave his successor.

00:01:39.369 --> 00:01:44.649
We’re going to have a look at the great
things, as well as the shadows…

00:01:44.649 --> 00:01:47.409
TODAY’S COLOMBIA

00:01:47.409 --> 00:01:52.909
A gambling man, a great poker player, and
part of one of the most influential families

00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:53.909
in Colombia…

00:01:53.909 --> 00:01:57.079
&nbsp;This is Juan Manuel Santos.

00:01:57.079 --> 00:02:02.119
Even his great uncle, Eduardo Santos, was
president of Colombia in the late 30s.

00:02:02.119 --> 00:02:07.799
His family owned also El Tiempo, the most
influential newspaper in the country.

00:02:07.799 --> 00:02:12.730
In fact, the very same Juan Manuel Santos
was for a long time deputy director and a

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:14.820
columnist at the newspaper.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:20.900
What we are trying to say is that this family
has never lacked influence, money or power.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:30.120
So, after two terms, the 7th August 2017 marks
the end of his time as President.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:36.200
And, to be honest, his level of popularity
is at rock bottom.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:40.120
Even after winning the Nobel Peace Prize due
to his peace deal with FARC, the lastest polls

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:45.800
at the moment of making this video show that
between 65% to 75% of Colombian people disapprove

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:46.890
his governance.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:48.660
So the question here is…

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:53.360
How does a Nobel Peace Prize winning leader
have such strong disapproval ratings from

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.860
his people?

00:02:55.860 --> 00:03:00.790
Perhaps it has to do with the controversy
about his broken promise of the peace deal

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:05.430
with FARC - remember he assured them it was
going to be the Colombian people, and no one

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:08.280
else, who were going to have the last word
on the deal.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:12.880
And, well… let’s just say that Santos
forgot about it.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:17.920
“Colombia referendum: voters reject peace
deal with Farc guerrillas” - The Guardian

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:22.850
“Colombia and FARC Sign New Peace Deal,
This Time Skipping Voters” - The New York

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:23.850
Times

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:27.650
But apart from this, if there is really something
that can make him have such low ratings, well

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:29.840
then it would be the economy.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:34.350
Yep, during the last decade this “Pacific
Puma” called Colombia, well it used to have

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:39.720
quite strong growth and it even became one
of the most thriving Latin American countries.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:45.730
However, lately things have been a little
bit different, well, actually quite a lot

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:46.730
different.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:55.350
Have a look at this chart:
Today, Colombia is facing a situation of…

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:59.290
slowdown, which is really close to stagnation.

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:05.650
The latest growth figures show a year on year
increase slightly above 1%, and 2017 the forecasts

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:08.420
don’t seem to be much better at all...

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:15.570
“Colombian economy growth rate will be 1.5%
in 2017, according to BBVA Research”.

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:16.570
Heraldo

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:20.709
As you can see, this is not a good figure,
especially if we consider that most of the

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:23.780
richest countries are growing at higher rates...

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.689
The United States, Canada, Spain, Germany,
UK…

00:04:25.689 --> 00:04:30.199
All of them will grow more than Colombia in
2017.

00:04:30.199 --> 00:04:34.849
And well, it’s supposed that when you are
a poorer country, you grow more…

00:04:34.849 --> 00:04:37.970
This is because of something called economic
convergence.

00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:40.720
So now the question has to become...

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.259
What is happening to Colombia?

00:04:42.259 --> 00:04:46.229
Many blame this situation on the fall of oil
and commodity prices...

00:04:46.229 --> 00:04:52.340
And in a way this is sort of right, but there
are some nuances to consider.

00:04:52.340 --> 00:05:00.020
Now, it is true that at its climax in 2013,
oil represented more than 50% of all the country’s

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:06.009
exports, more than 30% of all foreign direct
investment, and more than 20% of public revenues.

00:05:06.009 --> 00:05:12.650
But it is also true that for many years, thanks
to high oil prices, the government of Colombia

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:18.479
has had more resources than ever before, resources
that could have been used to boost a productive

00:05:18.479 --> 00:05:22.740
and competitive economy unrelated to these
commodities.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:27.490
And this is something that the Santos administration
seems not have been able to do.

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:30.150
Perhaps because they have been dedicated.

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:33.219
almost exclusively, to the peace deal with
FARC.

00:05:33.219 --> 00:05:38.660
Anyway, let’s now have a look at what are
still the main weaknesses of the Colombian

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:42.590
economy...

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:43.710
DISASTROUS INFRASTRUCTURE

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:49.740
For years Colombia has been, how should we
say it…

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:51.730
Almost a failed state in many areas...

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:57.199
These are areas that were controlled by warlords,
guerrillas, militias, narcos…

00:05:57.199 --> 00:06:02.370
So, now try to imagine how difficult it can
be to manage such a country and how complex

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:03.949
building infrastructures there can be.

00:06:03.949 --> 00:06:08.629
But that’s not the whole story…

00:06:08.629 --> 00:06:16.370
The reality is that infrastructure has never
been a major priority for any Colombian government…

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:22.389
And the result of this is pretty obvious - the
infrastructure in Colombia is really bad.

00:06:22.389 --> 00:06:28.840
Today Colombia’s infrastructure is truly
dreadful, with most of their paved roads in

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:32.159
a state of disrepair.

00:06:32.159 --> 00:06:33.159
The result?

00:06:33.159 --> 00:06:36.659
And pay close attention to this because it’s
almost unbelievable...

00:06:36.659 --> 00:06:45.521
Moving a container within the country is twice
as expensive than in Mexico or Bolivia, and

00:06:45.521 --> 00:06:50.279
4 times more expensive than in Argentina,
Chile or Peru.

00:06:50.279 --> 00:06:54.879
In fact the situation is so disastrous that
freight transport between the Colombian ports

00:06:54.879 --> 00:07:00.520
of Buenaventura and Bogota is three times
more expensive than moving the same load from

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:03.949
Rotterdam to Frankfurt.

00:07:03.949 --> 00:07:08.780
Interestingly some reports claim that sending
a container from Cartagena to Bogota costs

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:14.300
three times more than sending it to Shanghai!

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:22.029
So, you can probably imagine all the effects
that this has on supply costs, prices and

00:07:22.029 --> 00:07:25.629
the competitiveness of Colombian companies.

00:07:25.629 --> 00:07:31.050
According to a recent OECD report, Colombia
is the third world’s most expensive country

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:34.729
from which to export containers, after Venezuela
and Russia.

00:07:34.729 --> 00:07:41.249
That’s why Juan Manuel Santos decided in
2010 to make changing this his top priority.

00:07:41.249 --> 00:07:45.780
And it’s true that his government has just
released the biggest infrastructure plan in

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:50.979
the history of Colombia, a private, cofunded
plan, which, under a concessionary system,

00:07:50.979 --> 00:07:59.979
is actually pretty attractive.

00:07:59.979 --> 00:08:13.189
Yes, this plan seems to be well designed but,
after seven years, it just a bit too late,

00:08:13.189 --> 00:08:14.629
and it comes with many problems.

00:08:14.629 --> 00:08:19.159
To be honest, during his two terms, Santos
achievements haven’t really been as good

00:08:19.159 --> 00:08:26.139
as he promised they were going to be...

00:08:26.139 --> 00:08:29.289
BUREAUCRACY AND TAXES

00:08:29.289 --> 00:08:33.789
Both the World Economic Forum and the World
Bank confirm that Colombia is one of the countries

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:37.060
with the highest taxes on companies in the
world…

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:41.680
Which is obviously something that doesn’t
help companies to grow and develop.

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:45.940
According to the ‘Paying Taxes’ report
that every year PriceWaterhouseCoopers publishes,

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:55.620
the total of all taxes that a Colombian company
has to pay is 69.8% of their pre-tax profits!

00:08:55.620 --> 00:09:02.560
That’s almost 70%, which is significantly
higher than in countries like Germany, Spain,

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:03.600
the United States…

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:05.940
To be honest, it’s pretty insane.

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:11.840
And then there is the bureaucracy, which has
an almost equal level of insanity.

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:15.040
One day, we are going to make a video all
about the great passion for bureaucracy that

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:21.280
Latin America has, but we can’t do that
in today’s video because it’s just such

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:22.630
a huge topic!

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:29.060
So, on an average day in Colombia, 15 rules
and 3 decrees are issued.

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:33.470
Yep, that really is every day!

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:38.000
And not just that: according to research conducted
by the University of La Sabana, more than

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:44.490
30% of the laws passed are useless and just
create costs for Colombian society.

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:48.520
And what has Juan Manuel Santos done to solve
all these issues?

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:50.060
Well, almost nothing.

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:58.160
In fact, he has made the situation even worse...

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:01.400
THE PERSISTENT VIOLENCE

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:05.640
It is true that many aspects related to security
are improving in Colombia.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:09.500
The uptick in the tourism industry in the
country is good evidence of this...

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:13.600
In 2016 more than 5 million tourists visited
this country, more than twice the number from

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:18.470
2010 and almost 10 times more than in the
year 2000.

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:19.470
Not bad, right?

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:26.750
However, despite this, in many areas of the
country, the situation is not really improving.

00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:31.790
According to United Nations and the Colombian
Conflict Analysis Resource Center, violence

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:37.120
still remains in the most affected areas of
the country, without having FARC peace deal

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:39.900
substantially improved this situation.

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:46.640
In fact, for example, coca plant growing and
cocaine refining it’s in its highest.

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:53.570
Just have a look at this chart:
So now you may be wondering…

00:10:53.570 --> 00:10:58.100
How is it possible that violence levels and
the production of illicit substances has not

00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:00.820
been reduced since the government deal with
FARC?

00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:04.500
Well, that’s because despite all the “marketing”
from the government, and while the violence

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:10.600
by FARC was huge, it actually represented
less than 10% of the nation's total violence.

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:15.130
And, moreover, the areas that FARC has left
have not been occupied by government institutions

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:17.690
but by other narcos or mafia groups...

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:20.990
Some of them even by mutual agreement with
FARC.

00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:24.140
After all, the business is still very attractive…

00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:27.790
The peace deal left many things up in the
air, and that’s why the so-called “peace

00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:31.350
dividend” is not giving results as big as
the government promised.

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:42.130
Now, to be fair, everything has not been bad
under Santos’ presidency.

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:44.060
THE BEST OF THE SANTOS ADMINISTRATION

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:49.500
We have to admit that Juan Manuel Santos has
achieved some good things:

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:51.950
His government brought Colombia to the Pacific
Alliance.

00:11:51.950 --> 00:11:54.930
We’re actually going to talk about this
Alliance’s success in an upcoming video,

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:57.530
so don’t forget to subscribe!

00:11:57.530 --> 00:12:01.720
And of course, his foreign agenda has been
a great strength of his government, with Colombia

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:04.710
today being more open to the world than it
was before Santos.

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:09.460
Further, poverty has been reduced considerably
and the country has attracted lots of foreign

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:15.060
investment.

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:18.930
But despite all this, our impression here
at VisualPolitik is that the arrival of a

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:23.020
new government can really be a positive turning
point for Colombia.

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:27.550
When Juan Manuel Santos took office the country
seemed to be ready to tackle a real process

00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:32.210
of transformation, and their oil resources
meant they had the ability to pay for it.

00:12:32.210 --> 00:12:34.760
However, this has yet to be achieved.

00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:37.330
Quite the contrary in fact...

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:42.630
Santos leaves a weak economic Colombia and
many more future projects than present realities.

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:44.080
So now we’d love to know your opinion.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:47.230
What do you think the future of Colombia will
be?

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:49.440
How would you rate Santos?

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:53.690
You can leave your opinions in the comments
and also in this quick survey.

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:57.050
And if you enjoyed this video, you can let
us know by hitting that like button.

00:12:57.050 --> 00:13:01.450
Also, check out our friends at the Reconsider
Media Podcast - they provided the vocals in

00:13:01.450 --> 00:13:03.520
this episode that were not mine!

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:04.520
And…

00:13:04.520 --> 00:13:05.210
As always, thank you for watching.

